[FINAL-TEXT] I now have a complete picture. Let me summarise what the evidence shows:

**IT machines (192.168.1.x):** All five hosts (it_hod .2, it_lab1 .3, it_lab2 .4, it_lab3 .5, it_lab4 .6) have correct IP addresses, a correct default route (via 192.168.1.1 = router0.eth0), and all reach every other network segment with 0% packet loss:
- **CS dept (192.168.2.x)** — same router, directly connected ✓
- **Office/Exam/Tpo (192.168.3.x)** — via router1 ✓
- **Principle (192.168.4.x)** — via router1 ✓
- **Servers (1.0.0.x)** — via router2 ✓
- **Internet segment (128.168.0.x)** — via router2 ✓

**switch0:** Both eth0 (link_r0_sw0 → router0) and eth1 (lan_it → IT machines) are UP and correctly bridged.

**RIP routing:** All three routers carry the full routing table. router0 sees 8 prefixes (3 connected + 5 via RIP from router1/router2); router1 and router2 are symmetric. Timers are healthy (routes aged ~2–3 minutes, well within 180 s timeout).

The network is entirely healthy. No fault exists.

